"To determine the total distance the car travels from the time the brakes are applied to the time it stops, we need to sum the distances traveled in each second until the car comes to a complete stop.\n\n1. In the first second, the car travels 45 feet.\n2. In the second second, the car travels \\(45 - 5 = 40\\) feet.\n3. In the third second, the car travels \\(40 - 5 = 35\\) feet.\n4. In the fourth second, the car travels \\(35 - 5 = 30\\) feet.\n5. In the fifth second, the car travels \\(30 - 5 = 25\\) feet.\n6. In the sixth second, the car travels \\(25 - 5 = 20\\) feet.\n\nNow, we sum these distances:\n\\[\n45 + 40 + 35 + 30 + 25 + 20 = 215 \\text{ feet}\n\\]\n\nThe total distance the car travels from the time the brakes are applied to the time it stops is \\(\\boxed{215}\\) feet."
"Let the three consecutive integers be \\( x \\), \\( x+1 \\), and \\( x+2 \\). According to the problem, the sum of these integers is 27. Therefore, we can write the equation:\n\n\\[ x + (x+1) + (x+2) = 27 \\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\n\\[ 3x + 3 = 27 \\]\n\nSubtracting 3 from both sides, we have:\n\n\\[ 3x = 24 \\]\n\nDividing both sides by 3, we find:\n\n\\[ x = 8 \\]\n\nSo, the three consecutive integers are 8, 9, and 10. To find the product of these three integers, we multiply them together:\n\n\\[ 8 \\times 9 \\times 10 \\]\n\nCalculating the product, we get:\n\n\\[ 8 \\times 9 = 72 \\]\n\\[ 72 \\times 10 = 720 \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the integers is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{720}\\)."
