"To expand \\((x+5)^2\\), we can use the binomial theorem. The binomial theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n\\]\n\nIn this case, \\(a = x\\) and \\(b = 5\\). Applying the binomial theorem, we get:\n\n\\[\n(x + 5)^2 = x^2 + 2 \\cdot x \\cdot 5 + 5^2\n\\]\n\nNow, let's simplify each term:\n\n\\[\nx^2 = x^2\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\cdot x \\cdot 5 = 10x\n\\]\n\\[\n5^2 = 25\n\\]\n\nPutting it all together, we have:\n\n\\[\n(x + 5)^2 = x^2 + 10x + 25\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the expanded form of \\((x+5)^2\\) is \\(\\boxed{x^2 + 10x + 25}\\).\n\nFinal: The final answer is \\(x^2 + 10x + 25$. I hope it is correct."
"To find the greatest possible value of the slope of the line containing points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\), we need to consider the coordinates of these points. The slope of a line passing through two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{slope} = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}\n\\]\n\nGiven the coordinates of points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\), we can calculate the slope as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\text{slope} = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the coordinates of points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{slope} = \\frac{3 - 2}{5 - 4} = \\frac{1}{1} = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the greatest possible value of the slope of the line containing points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]"
