"To determine the smallest possible integer value for \\( x \\) such that \\( f(x) \\) \\in \\real{, we need to find the domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2x-6}}{x-3} \\).\n\n1. The expression inside the square root, \\( 2x - 6 \\), must be non-negative:\n   \\[\n   2x - 6 \\geq 0 \\implies 2x \\geq 6 \\implies x \\geq 3\n   \\]\n\n2. The denominator, \\( x - 3 \\), must not be zero:\n   \\[\n   x - 3 \\neq 0 \\implies x \\neq 3\n   \\]\n\nCombining these two conditions, we get:\n\\[\nx \\geq 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x \\neq 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the domain of the function \\( f(x) \\) is \\( x \\in \\real) - 3 \\).\n\nThe smallest possible integer value for \\( x \\) in this domain is \\( x = 4 \\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{4}\\)."
