"To determine the number of three-element sets \\(\\{a, b, c\\}\\) of distinct positive integers such that \\(a \\times b \\times c = 2310\\), we start by finding the prime factorization of 2310.\n\n\\[\n2310 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5 \\times 7 \\times 11\n\\]\n\nThe prime factors of 2310 are 2, 3, 5, 7, and 11. We need to find the number of ways to choose 3 distinct factors from these 5 factors. This is a combination problem, and we use the combination formula \\(\\binom{n}{k}\\), where \\(n\\) is the total number of items and \\(k\\) is the number of items to choose.\n\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{3} = \\frac{5!}{3!(5-3)!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4 \\times 3}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 10\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of three-element sets of distinct positive integers that satisfy the equation is \\(\\boxed{10}\\)."
"First, we determine the total number of possible outcomes when rolling three fair dice. Each die has 6 faces, so the total number of outcomes is \\(6^3 = 216\\).\n\nNext, we calculate the number of outcomes where at least two dice show a 1. We can use the complement rule to make this easier. The number of outcomes where none of the dice show a 1 is the number of outcomes where each die shows a number other than 1. Each die has 5 faces (2, 3, 4, 5, 6), so the number of outcomes where none of the dice show a 1 is \\(5^3 = 125\\).\n\nTherefore, the number of outcomes where at least two dice show a 1 is \\(216 - 125 = 91\\).\n\nThe probability that rolling at least two 1's is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of outcomes:\n\n$$\\frac{91}{216} = \\frac{17}{36}.$$\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\frac{17}{36}\\). I hope it is correct."
"To determine the number of ways to select 4 members from a team of 8 members, we use the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\), where \\( n \\) is the total number of items, and \\( k \\) is the number of items to choose.\n\nHere, \\( n = 8 \\) and \\( k = 4 \\).\n\n\\[\n\\binom{8}{4} = \\frac{8!}{4!(8-4)!} = \\frac{8!}{4!4!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the common terms in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{8!}{4!4!} = \\frac{8 \\times 7 \\times 6 \\times 5 \\times 4!}{4! \\times 4!} = \\frac{8 \\times 7 \\times 6 \\times 5}{4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 70\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to select 4 members from a math team of 8 members is \\(\\boxed{70}\\)."
