"To determine the probability that Polya's next five stops are on the \\(7^{\\text{th}}\\) floor or higher, we need to consider the possible outcomes of the coin flips and their effects on her position.\n\nPolya starts on the \\(6^{\\text{th}}\\) floor. She flips a fair coin five times. Each flip can be either heads (H) or tails (T). Therefore, there are \\(2^5 = 32\\) possible outcomes of the coin flips.\n\nFor Polya to be on the \\(7^{\\text{th}}\\) floor or higher after five flips, she must end up on the \\(7^{\\text{th}}\\) floor. This means she must go up exactly one floor in five flips. The only way to achieve this is if she flips heads exactly once and tails exactly four times.\n\nThere is only one such sequence: TTHTT.\n\nThus, the probability that Polya's next five stops are on the \\(7^{\\text{th}}\\) floor or higher is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{32}\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{32}}\\)."
"To find the probability that a 5 is rolled on at least one of the two dice, we can use the complementary probability approach. First, we calculate the probability that a 5 is not rolled on either die.\n\nThe probability of not rolling a 5 on a single die is \\(\\frac{5}{6}\\).\n\nSince the dice are independent, the probability of not rolling a 5 on both dice is:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right) \\times \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right) = \\frac{25}{36}\n\\]\n\nNow, the probability that a 5 is rolled on at least one of the two dice is the complement of the probability that a 5 is not rolled on either die:\n\\[\n1 - \\frac{25}{36} = \\frac{36}{36} - \\frac{25}{36} = \\frac{11}{36}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{11}{36}}\\). \n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\frac{11}{36}\\). I hope it is correct."
