"To find the least upper bound on the ratio \\(\\frac{\\text{volume of } P}{\\text{surface area of } P}\\) for a polyhedron \\(P\\) inscribed in a sphere of radius \\(36\\), we can use the known result from geometry and calculus.\n\nThe least upper bound on the ratio \\(\\frac{\\text{volume of } P}{\\text{surface area of } P}\\) for a polyhedron inscribed in a sphere of radius \\(R\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{volume of } P}{\\text{surface area of } P} \\leq \\frac{R^3}{3}\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(R = 36\\), we substitute this value into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{volume of } P}{\\text{surface area of } P} \\leq \\frac{36^3}{3} = \\frac{46656}{3} = 1552\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the least upper bound on the ratio \\(\\frac{\\text{volume of } P}{\\text{surface area of } P}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1552}\n\\]"
"To compute \\(\\tan 150^\\circ\\), we can use the fact that the tangent function is periodic with a period of \\(180^\\circ\\). This means that \\(\\tan(\\theta + 180^\\circ) = \\tan(\\theta)\\).\n\nFirst, we express \\(150^\\circ\\) in terms of an angle within the interval \\([0^\\circ, 180^\\circ)\\):\n\\[\n150^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 30^\\circ\n\\]\n\nUsing the periodicity of the tangent function, we have:\n\\[\n\\tan 150^\\circ = \\tan(180^\\circ - 30^\\circ) = \\tan 30^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\tan 30^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\tan 150^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}}\\)\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\). I hope it is correct."
