"To solve the equation \\((x - 1) + (x - 2) + (x - 3) + (x - 4) = x\\), we first simplify the left-hand side of the equation.\n\nCombine like terms:\n\\[\n(x - 1) + (x - 2) + (x - 3) + (x - 4) = 4x - 10\n\\]\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n4x - 10 = x\n\\]\n\nNext, we isolate \\(x\\) by subtracting \\(x\\) from both sides:\n\\[\n4x - x - 10 = x - x\n\\]\n\\[\n3x - 10 = 0\n\\]\n\nThen, we add 10 to both sides:\n\\[\n3x - 10 + 10 = 0 + 10\n\\]\n\\[\n3x = 10\n\\]\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by 3:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{10}{3}\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\boxed{3}\n\\]\nboxedboxed{3}}\\]"
"To find out how much of the pie Louis and Jack eat altogether, we need to add the fractions of the pie they each eat.\n\nLouis eats \\(\\frac{1}{5}\\) of the pie, and Jack eats \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\) of the pie.\n\nFirst add the fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nTo add these fractions, we need a common denominator. The least common multiple of 5 and 3 is 15.\n\nConvert each fraction to have this common denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{3}{15}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{10}{15}\n\\]\n\nNow add the fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{15} + \\frac{10}{15} = \\frac{13}{15}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Louis and Jack eat together \\(\\boxed{\\frac{13}{15}}\\) of the pie altogether.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\frac{13}{15}\\). I hope it is correct."
