"To calculate \\((.\\overline{6})(3)\\), we first need to express the repeating decimal \\(0.\\overline{6}\\) as a fraction. Let \\(x = 0.\\overline{6}\\).\n\nTo eliminate the repeating decimal, we multiply both sides by 10:\n\\[10x = 6.\\overline{6}\\]\n\nNow, we subtract the original equation \\(x = 0.\\overline{6}\\) from this new equation:\n\\[10x - x = 6.\\overline{6} - 0.\\overline{6}\\]\n\\[9x = 6\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\):\n\\[x = \\frac{6}{9}\\]\n\nSo, \\(0.\\overline{6} = \\frac{6}{9}\\).\n\nNext, we multiply \\(\\frac{6}{9}\\) by 3:\n\\[\\frac{6}{9} \\times 3 = \\frac{6 \\times 3}{9} = \\frac{18}{9} = \\frac{2}{3}\\]\n\nFinal Answer:\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{\\frac{2}{3}}\\]"
"To solve the inequality \\(3(x-5) \\le 7\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Distribute the 3 on the left side of the inequality:\n   \\[\n   3x - 15 \\le 7\n   \\]\n\n2. Add 15 to both sides of the inequality to isolate the term with \\(x\\):\n   \\[\n   3x - 15 + 15 \\le 7 + 15\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   3x \\le 22\n   \\]\n\n3. Divide both sides of the inequality by 3 to solve for \\(x\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3x}{3} \\le \\frac{22}{3}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   x \\le 7\n   \\]\n\nThe positive integer solutions to \\(x \\le 7\\) are \\(1, 2, 3, 4, 7\\).\n\nTherefore, the number of positive integer solutions is \\(\\boxed{5}\\)."
