"To determine how much Anna pays for her fudge, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the cost of \\(\\frac{7}{9}\\) of a pound of fudge.\n2. Round the cost to the nearest cent.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the cost of \\(\\frac{7}{9}\\) of a pound of fudge. The cost per pound is $10, so the cost for \\(\\frac{7}{9}\\) of a pound is:\n\n\\[\n10 \\times \\frac{7}{9} = \\frac{70}{9}\n\\]\n\nNext, we convert the fraction to a decimal:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{70}{9} \\approx 7.7778\n\\]\n\nNow, we round $7.7778 to the nearest cent. The digit in the hundredths place is 7, and the digit in the thousandths place is 7. Since 7 is greater than 5, we round up the hundredths place:\n\n\\[\n7.7778\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Anna pays \\(\\boxed{7.78}\\) dollars for her fudge."
"Let the smaller number be \\( x \\) and the larger number be \\( y \\).\n\nWe are given two equations:\n1. \\( x + y = 15 \\)\n2. \\( 4x = 2y - 60 \\)\n\nFirst, solve the first equation for \\( y \\):\n\\[ y = 15 - x \\]\n\nNext, substitute \\( y = 15 - x \\) into the second equation:\n\\[ 4x = 2(15 - x) - 60 \\]\n\\[ 4x = 30 - 2x - 60 \\]\n\\[ 4x = -30 - 2x \\]\n\\[ 6x = -30 \\]\n\\[ x = -5 \\]\n\nNow, substitute \\( x = -5 \\) back into the equation for \\( y \\):\n\\[ y = 15 - (-5) \\]\n\\[ y = 15 + 5 \\]\n\\[ y = 20 \\]\n\nTherefore, the larger number is 20.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is 20. I hope it is correct."
