"To determine the probability that all four coins (a penny, a nickel, a dime, and a quarter) come up heads when flipped simultaneously, we need to consider the probability of each individual coin showing heads and then multiply these probabilities together.\n\nEach coin has two possible outcomes: heads (H) or tails (T). The probability of getting heads on a single coin flip is \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n\nSince the flips of the coins are independent events, the probability of all four coins showing heads is the product of the probabilities of each individual coin showing heads.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Probability} = \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^4 = \\frac{1}{16}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that all four coins come up heads is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{16}}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{16}}\\)."
