"The polar curve \\( r = 1 + \\cos \\theta \\) is a cardioid. When this curve is rotated around the point \\((2,0)\\), it forms a sector of a circle with radius 2.\n\nThe area of a sector of a circle is given by the formula:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} r^2 \\theta\n\\]\nwhere \\( r \\) is the radius and \\( \\theta \\) is the central angle in radians.\n\nIn this case, the radius \\( r \\) is 2, and the central angle \\( \\theta \\) is \\( \\pi \\) radians (since the curve is rotated once around the point \\((2,0)\\)).\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 2^2 \\times \\pi = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4 \\times \\pi = 2\\pi\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the region it over is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\pi\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\( 2\\pi \\). I hope it is correct."
"To find \\(\\cot \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\), we need to recall the definition of the cotangent function. The cotangent of an angle \\(\\theta\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\cot \\theta = \\frac{1}{\\tan \\theta}\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\) is equivalent to \\(270^\\circ\\) in radians. The tangent of \\(270^\\circ\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\tan 270^\\circ = 0\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the cotangent of \\(270^\\circ\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\cot 270^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\tan 270^\\circ} = \\frac{1}{0} = \\infty\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\cot \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\infty}\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\infty\\). I hope it is correct."
