"To find the smallest \\( x \\)-coordinate of any point on the graph defined by \\( r = \\cos \\theta + \\frac{1}{2} \\), we need to understand the relationship between polar and Cartesian coordinates.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the equation \\( r = \\cos \\theta + \\frac{1}{2} \\) can be converted to Cartesian coordinates using the relationships:\n\\[ x = r \\cos \\theta \\]\n\\[ y = r \\sin \\theta \\]\n\nGiven \\( r = \\cos \\theta + \\frac{1}{2} \\), we substitute \\( r \\) into the equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = (\\cos \\theta + \\frac{1}{2}) \\cos \\theta \\]\n\\[ x = \\cos^2 \\theta + \\frac{1}{2} \\]\n\nThe minimum value of \\( \\cos^2 \\theta \\) is 0, so the minimum value of \\( x \\) is:\n\\[ x_{\\text{min}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\]\n\nTherefore, the smallest \\( x \\)-coordinate of any point on the graph is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\]"
"We can simplify the given expression using the angle addition and subtraction formulas. The numerator can be rewritten as $\\cos (5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) + \\cos (35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) - \\cos 25^\\circ - \\cos 85^\\circ$. The denominator can be rewritten as $\\sin (5^\\circ + 20^\\circ) - \\sin (35^\\circ + 50^\\circ) + \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ$. Therefore, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{\\cos 25^\\circ + \\cos 85^\\circ - \\cos 25^\\circ - \\cos 85^\\circ}{\\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ + \\sin 25^\\circ - \\sin 85^\\circ} = \\frac{-\\cos 65^\\circ}{-\\sin 65^\\circ} = \\cot 65^\\circ$. Therefore, the least positive value of $\\theta$ is $\\boxed{65^\\circ}$.\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $65^\\circ$. I hope it is correct."
"We can write the system of equations in matrix form as:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n1 & k & -1 \\\\\nk & -1 & -1 \\\\\n1 & 1 & k\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nx \\\\\ny \\\\\nz\n\\end{pmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n0 \\\\\n0 \\\\\n0\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nFor the system to have a non-trivial solution, the coefficient matrix must be singular. This means that the determinant of the matrix must be zero.\n\nWe compute the determinant of the matrix:\n\\[\n\\det \\begin{pmatrix}\n1 & k & -1 \\\\\nk & -1 & -1 \\\\\n1 & 1 & k\n\\end{pmatrix}\n= (1)(-1)(k) - (k)(-1)(1) + (-1)(k)(1)\n= -k + k^2 - k\n= k^2 - k\n\\]\nSetting the determinant equal to zero, we get:\n\\[\nk^2 - k = 0\n\\]\n\\[\nk(k-1) = 0\n\\]\nThus, $k=\\boxed{0,1}$."
